COMPUTED_KEYWORD_GAPS was measured by code with literal ATS matching and is
authoritative; do not re-derive coverage. Use it this way:
- `stated_in_master_but_missing_from_draft`: the candidate has this evidence
  but the draft never says it. Fix these first with section edits, using the
  exact spelling, inside bullets whose source facts support them.
- `gaps_whose_proxy_evidence_is_missing_from_draft`: the closest real evidence
  for a missing requirement never reached the page. Name the proxy technology
  where its fact is already allocated.
- `never_claim_in_bullets`: technologies the candidate has not used. They must
  not appear in any work or project text; any occurrence is a truth violation.
- `bullets_naming_technology_their_facts_lack`: replace the named technology
  with the one the bullet's source facts actually state (e.g. "SQL", not
  "PostgreSQL") through a section edit.
- `placement_findings`: fix where possible without breaking counts or
  provenance.
Report `coverage_pct` equal to the computed figure unless your edits change it.

Return coverage, unresolved truth violations, recommendations, and optional
complete replacements for named sections. Evaluate the resume as if every
proposed edit were applied. If an edit would still leave an unsupported claim,
include that claim in truth_violations.

`truth_violations` is the post-edit blocking list, not an audit history. When a
valid section edit removes or corrects a claim, do not also return that old claim
as a truth violation. Put the correction rationale in recommendations instead.
Every truth-violation `claim` must quote text that exists verbatim in the
post-edit resume; never reconstruct, combine, or paraphrase a claim from STAR
metadata.

Section edit contracts:
- skills: the same compact skills response used by the skills stage; you may
  include JD must-have and nice-to-have keywords the master lacks, but never
  repeat a keyword across groups
- work: the same complete source_id/summary/relevance/STAR-bullets response used
  by the work stage; preserve each evidence_assignment_id, its allocated global
  source_ids, inferred impact claim, and the 4/3/3 counts; never return employer,
  title, dates, or URL
- projects: the same compact source_id/description/relevance/bullets response
  used by the project stage; preserve two STAR bullets per project with local
  provenance; never return name, dates, URL, or technologies

Return ONLY:
{
  "coverage_pct": 0,
  "missing_must_haves": [],
  "weak_sections": [],
  "truth_violations": [
    {"section": "work", "claim": "exact unsupported claim", "reason": "why unsupported"}
  ],
  "recommendations": [],
  "notes": null,
  "section_edits": null
}

Audit every planned and edited claim. A contradictory attribution, missing STAR
element, duplicated accomplishment, unallocated provenance, changed count, or
protected-field change is a truth violation. Flag an inferred metric only when
it is egregiously inflated or grounded in no source fact; when in doubt, put it
in recommendations, not truth_violations. Any unresolved truth violation blocks
the run.

Audit bullet quality per the bullet guide and verb dictionary, but put style
findings in recommendations, never truth_violations: bullets opening with
"responsible for," "worked on," "helped with," or "participated in"; restricted
verbs (Led, Owned, Architected, Optimized, Scaled, Automated, Streamlined,
Launched, Deployed, Transformed, Drove, Spearheaded) without supporting
evidence; ownership verbs stronger than the ownership-calibration ladder
allows; and three or more bullets in one section opening with the same verb.

Also audit voice, and report these in recommendations:
- Three or more bullets in one section sharing the same clause structure, or
  more than one bullet in a role ending with a trailing participial result
  clause (", enabling ...", ", establishing ...", ", reducing ..."). A role
  whose bullets share one rhythm reads as generated, not as work someone did.
- A verb whose strength exceeds the scope it describes ("architected" for a
  handful of endpoints), including a strong verb paired with a weak one.
- A multi-word technical noun phrase that names a category instead of the thing
  built, and that a non-expert reader could not picture.
- A number with no baseline or denominator, decimal precision on an estimate,
  more than one quantified claim in a bullet, or an absolute qualifier
  ("zero", "no tuning required", "flawless", "seamless").
- A role with no metric at all while a verified number for that role exists in
  the evidence plan, and the reverse: a role where every bullet ends in a
  statistic and none shows a decision, tradeoff, or obstacle.
- A technology named in a work or project bullet that is missing from the
  skills section, where it is invisible to keyword matching.
